<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kahal Zur Israel Synagogue</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1636</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kahal Zur Israel Synagogue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, founded around 1636 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule, was the first synagogue in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Its congregation was formed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sephardic-jews">Sephardic Jews</a>, many of Portuguese origin, who came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. When the Portuguese retook Recife in 1654 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> left, and some went on to found the first Jewish community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>. The restored building is now a museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kahal-zur-israel-synagogue.jpg" alt="Kahal Zur Israel Synagogue" width="320" height="282" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilfredor · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sinagoga_Kahal_Zur_Israel,_Recife,_Pernambuco,_Brasil.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
